National 30-m Forest Attribute Mapping across China
Overview
This repository contains the custom Python, R, and Google Earth Engine scripts used to support the national-scale 30-m mapping and analysis of multiple forest attributes across China. The workflow covers remote-sensing data acquisition, forest-boundary processing, data preparation, feature engineering, model development, national wall-to-wall prediction, validation, uncertainty analysis, driver analysis, and manuscript figure generation.
The repository was prepared to support manuscript peer review and to document the computational workflow used in the study.
Repository Structure
The main folders are organized by analysis stage:
- Google earth engine/
  Contains Google Earth Engine scripts for Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data acquisition and preprocessing, as well as forest-boundary preparation and related spatial processing.
- metadata/
  Contains metadata utilities, including standardized climate-zone names and abbreviations used throughout the workflow.
- data_preparation/
  Contains scripts for clipping and converting raster data, extracting predictor values at forest inventory plots, processing bioclimatic variables, cropping external forest products, and preparing source datasets.
- feature_engineering/
  Contains scripts for calculating vegetation indices, texture features, climate-zone assignments, and stacked predictor rasters.
- modeling/
  Contains scripts for generating cross-validation folds and training XGBoost models for forest attribute estimation.
- mapping/
  Contains scripts for national-scale prediction, GPU-based wall-to-wall mapping, raster mosaicking, visualization, and uncertainty mapping.
- validation/
  Contains Python and R scripts for statistical evaluation and observed-versus-predicted validation.
- additional_analysis/
  Contains scripts for climate-zone statistics, external product comparison, and additional summary analyses.
- driver_analysis/
  Contains scripts for preparing driving-variable datasets, calculating variance inflation factors, fitting driver-analysis models, and evaluating their performance.
- figures/
  Contains scripts used to generate manuscript figures, including validation plots, climate-zone summaries, feature-importance figures, SHAP plots, and product-comparison figures.
General Workflow
1. Remote-sensing data acquisition and forest-boundary processing
   Use Google Earth Engine to acquire and preprocess Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 imagery and to prepare the forest-boundary layer used to constrain subsequent analyses.
2. Data preparation
   Prepare forest inventory data and remote-sensing/environmental predictor layers, crop or align raster products, and extract predictor values at sample locations.
3. Feature engineering
   Derive vegetation indices, image-texture metrics, climate-zone information, and analysis-ready raster stacks.
4. Model development
   Create cross-validation folds and train predictive models for forest attributes.
5. National mapping
   Apply trained models to wall-to-wall predictor data, generate national 30-m forest attribute maps, mosaic prediction tiles, and calculate mapping uncertainty.
6. Validation
   Evaluate model performance using statistical metrics and observed-versus-predicted comparisons.
7. Additional analyses
   Summarize forest attributes by climate zone and compare mapped products with external datasets.
8. Driver analysis
   Examine relationships between forest attributes and environmental or climatic drivers using dedicated modeling and interpretation workflows.
9. Figure generation
   Produce the figures used for model evaluation, spatial analysis, product comparison, and driver interpretation.
Google Earth Engine Processing
The Google earth engine/ folder contains scripts used for the preparation of satellite remote-sensing inputs. These scripts mainly include:
- Sentinel-1 data acquisition and preprocessing;
- Sentinel-2 data acquisition and preprocessing;
- generation of seasonal or composite remote-sensing products where required;
- forest-boundary preparation and masking;
- spatial export of analysis-ready remote-sensing layers for subsequent local processing and modeling.
The Google Earth Engine scripts are intended to prepare the remote-sensing inputs used by the downstream Python and R workflows.
Software Environment
The local workflow uses both Python and R, while satellite data preprocessing is partly conducted in Google Earth Engine. Environment specifications are provided in:
- environment.example.yml
- requirements.txt
The Python workflow includes packages such as NumPy, pandas, Rasterio, GDAL, GeoPandas, scikit-learn, XGBoost, Optuna, SHAP, Matplotlib, Cartopy, SciPy, and related geospatial/scientific libraries.
Some scripts use GPU acceleration, so a compatible CUDA environment may be required for full national-scale execution.
Reproducibility
The scripts document the analysis logic used in the study. Full national-scale reproduction requires the original input datasets, sufficient storage and memory, access to Google Earth Engine for the remote-sensing preprocessing steps, and, for some local modeling and mapping steps, GPU computing resources.
Before running the scripts, users may need to:
- update local file paths;
- provide the required forest inventory and remote-sensing datasets;
- configure or adapt the Google Earth Engine assets and export destinations;
- reconstruct the Python/R software environment;
- ensure that raster projections, resolutions, extents, and naming conventions match those expected by the scripts.
Data Availability
Large raster predictors, raw remote-sensing data, trained models, logs, restricted forest inventory records, and restricted plot coordinates are not included in this repository.
The repository therefore focuses on providing the analysis code and computational workflow rather than redistributing large or restricted source datasets.
